From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] hdmitx21: vrr: refuse a rate before touching VRR, and leave
 it cleanly

A vrr_rate write the mode cannot take (under 23.97 Hz, over the mode's
rate, or with vrr_mode not game or QMS) went through
hdmitx_set_fr_hint_locked(), which switches VRR off first and then
returned 0, and the store returned success: VRR was left half on, the
VRR packet still sent, the line count frozen at the stretched value and
vinfo still at the old rate. The attribute now checks the rate first and
returns -EINVAL, VRR untouched.

Going back to rate 0 gave back vinfo's rate but not the line count, so
leaving game VRR without a mode set (Kodi quitting, its crash recovery,
a film with no usable multiple) left ENCP_VIDEO_MAX_LNCNT stretched; it
now restores the mode's v_total. The VRR packet is cleared when VRR was
in use even if vrr_mode already went to none, and a mode with no rate
(after a hotplug dropped it) keeps vinfo's rate rather than setting 0.

Both wait for a vsync handler already running (synchronize_irq) after
switching VRR off, so it cannot write the stretched line count and the
VRR packet back after the restore.

The DRM path (hdmitx_set_fr_hint) keeps its behaviour; the rate limit
moves to hdmitx_vrr_max_rate(), shared by both.

Every refusal is checked before VRR is touched: a QMS rate or base outside
its table, vrr_mode none, a rate out of range or needing too many lines,
and no output (not ready, suspended). The attribute returns -EINVAL,
-ERANGE or -EBUSY with VRR as it was; DRM, which has no error path, leaves
VRR as rate 0 does and gets the error, instead of VRR left half on.

Leaving VRR from outside a set (plugout, early suspend and mode disable,
through disable_21_work) now goes through the same stop and restore under
fr_hint_mutex, after the handler is done: those paths hold hdmimode_mutex,
which a set holds too, and a set refuses a rate without output, so a
vrr_rate write cannot turn VRR back on behind them. Their callers clear
the packets. The reboot notifier takes no lock: it stops VRR and waits for
a running handler once ready is cleared, and a set enabling VRR checks
ready again after enabling it (a full barrier on both sides).

The empty VTEM sent when VRR ends now goes out for 4 frames, then the
vsync handler clears the VTEM slot, as a mode set clears it, instead of
sending it until the next mode set.
Neither the empty VTEM nor that countdown is sent without output (not
ready, or suspended): its packets were cleared, and the HDMI TX clocks
may be gated while the vsync interrupt still runs. The reboot notifier
clears ready with WRITE_ONCE.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/media/vout/hdmitx21/hdmi_tx.h b/drivers/media/vout/hdmitx21/hdmi_tx.h
index 3ad870c..b5c5284 100644
--- a/drivers/media/vout/hdmitx21/hdmi_tx.h
+++ b/drivers/media/vout/hdmitx21/hdmi_tx.h
@@ -465,6 +465,8 @@ ssize_t _vrr_cap_show(struct device *dev, struct device_attribute *attr,
 int hdmitx_dump_vrr_status(struct seq_file *s, void *p);
 void hdmitx_vrr_enable(void);
 void hdmitx_vrr_disable(void);
+void hdmitx_vrr_off(void);
+void hdmitx_vrr_shutdown(void);
 
 /* mute realted */
 extern unsigned long avmute_ms;
